# ★Batch files MUST be CRLF (do not remove)★
#   cmd.exe re-seeks the file by byte offset after every executed line, assuming CRLF. An LF-only
#   .bat drifts one byte per line and eventually starts executing the MIDDLE OF A COMMENT as a
#   command — `cd /d "%~dp0.."` itself never runs, so every guard the comments describe is void.
#   Measured 2026-08-17: two launchers written with LF failed exactly this way; the same bytes with
#   CRLF ran correctly.
#   Without this rule the breakage is machine-dependent (core.autocrlf), so it survives review on
#   one checkout and reappears on another.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# PowerShell is tolerant of LF, but these sit next to the launchers and are edited together —
# keeping them uniform avoids mixed-ending diffs in the same directory.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
